WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.670
- I'm here hanging with
former first lady, Laura Bush,

00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:04.940
who's on the line from Texas.

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:07.200
So Mrs. Bush, your daughter,
Barbara and her husband

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:09.030
ended up moving home to Texas

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:11.140
while everyone was in
quarantine just for a bit.

00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:13.450
So what did you do with
the family back home?

00:00:13.450 --> 00:00:15.160
- She was in graduate school

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:18.200
and her graduate school had gone virtual.

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:21.130
So she was on Zoom, of course, all day.

00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:23.690
But we were at the ranch. This was sort of

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:27.580
at the very beginning
of the COVID shutdown.

00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:29.120
and so,

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:31.470
so we went for long walks.

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:34.040
That's what we love to do,
and then we watched a lot of

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:37.090
different series on television.

00:00:37.090 --> 00:00:37.923
- Oh, definitely.

00:00:37.923 --> 00:00:39.380
- That's our new entertainment.

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:42.530
- Oh, absolutely. Netflix. It was a wonder

00:00:42.530 --> 00:00:46.360
during the pandemic, so it helped us all.

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:49.080
So what's your favorite
way to spoil Jenna's kids?

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:51.100
And what does Jenna
have to say about that?

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:53.190
Y'all got all that hang time.

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:56.470
- Yeah, exactly. Well, our
favorite thing to do with them

00:00:56.470 --> 00:00:59.310
which I don't know if
she's thrilled about,

00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:01.610
but we love for them to come in early

00:01:01.610 --> 00:01:03.980
in the morning and get in bed with us.

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:05.740
I don't know when we're
staying together, I don't know

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:08.830
if you ever saw those old
photographs of the Bushes

00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:12.390
in bed with everyone, the
entire family, all their kids

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:16.560
around them in Maine, but we've
carried on that tradition.

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:19.770
So Mila and Poppy come
get in bed very early,

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:22.920
before Jen and Henry are
up, and then of course

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:27.630
what they want to do is get
our iPads and work on them.

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:32.360
So now my iPad, my wallpaper
is a picture of my two cats.

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:34.490
Mila knew how to do that.

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:37.250
Another background is
a picture of our dog.

00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:39.870
Mila knew how to set it up that way.

00:01:39.870 --> 00:01:42.100
So it's actually been an advantage

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:44.390
for them to play on my iPad.

00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:47.080
- Oh my gosh, my children as well, I'm 39

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:49.670
but my six year old can
navigate it better than I can.

00:01:49.670 --> 00:01:52.314
It's crazy, and I'm like, how
do you know this password?

00:01:52.314 --> 00:01:54.098
And she, somehow, she knows it.

00:01:54.098 --> 00:01:57.950
But do you have any favorite
Texas family traditions?

00:01:57.950 --> 00:01:59.830
- Texas family traditions?

00:01:59.830 --> 00:02:02.080
We go to the Texas state fair.

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:03.450
- Oh, well that's a given.

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:05.380
Yes. Do you eat fried there?

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:07.000
Do you eat like the funnel cakes?

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:07.973
Do you go for it?

00:02:09.370 --> 00:02:10.203
- No, not really.

00:02:10.203 --> 00:02:12.680
But one year when Barbara
and Jenna were little

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:15.220
and we lived in Dallas and we went

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:17.900
to the Texas state fair
and Jenna was chosen to

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:21.850
be the red pig cheerleader
at the pig races.

00:02:21.850 --> 00:02:23.376
That was amusing.

00:02:23.376 --> 00:02:25.490
(laughter)

00:02:25.490 --> 00:02:29.600
- I am totally bringing that
up the next time we talk.

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:31.290
Oh, that's awesome.

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:33.140
But since we're both Texans

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:35.620
I think we have time for a quick game

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:37.303
we are calling Lone Star Love.

00:02:37.303 --> 00:02:38.868
(upbeat country music)

00:02:38.868 --> 00:02:40.035
Lone Star Love

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:44.390
I'll say something that's truly Texan

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:46.330
and we will share if we've ever done it.

00:02:46.330 --> 00:02:47.510
Here we go.

00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:49.600
Had a family member who created their own

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:51.215
homemade barbecue sauce.

00:02:51.215 --> 00:02:53.270
(laughs)

00:02:53.270 --> 00:02:56.350
- No, I haven't had that
but my dad's best friend

00:02:56.350 --> 00:02:58.980
in Midland owned Johnny's barbecue.

00:02:58.980 --> 00:02:59.813
- Oh!

00:02:59.813 --> 00:03:01.310
- The barbecue joint in Midland.

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:04.120
So he spent a lot of time at,

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:06.774
at Johnny's barbecue and he
would mainly pick up and,

00:03:06.774 --> 00:03:09.250
bring it home for us to eat at home.

00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:10.410
- Oh, that's awesome.

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:12.010
I don't have anybody in my family

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:14.620
but I definitely have a couple of friends.

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:17.294
Okay. So have you ever told
someone it's pronounced

00:03:17.294 --> 00:03:19.010
(laughs)

00:03:19.010 --> 00:03:21.683
told someone it's
pronounced pecan, not pecan.

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:24.820
- No, but I've always used pecan.

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:25.980
- Me too,

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:26.813
and everybody, yeah

00:03:26.813 --> 00:03:28.640
I think it's like any Georgia friend

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:31.690
or anybody, they say pecan,
and I'm like, "It's pecan".

00:03:31.690 --> 00:03:33.563
It's not tomato, tomato. It's pecan.

00:03:34.544 --> 00:03:35.410
- [Laura] It's pecan.

00:03:35.410 --> 00:03:36.770
- [Kelly] It's pecan.

00:03:36.770 --> 00:03:38.550
We made it so.

00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:40.732
Okay. So have--I say this all the time.

00:03:40.732 --> 00:03:42.680
Have you ever said "All y'all"

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:44.330
because "Y'all" just isn't enough?

00:03:44.330 --> 00:03:45.193
I, yeah.

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:46.993
You probably don't.

00:03:46.993 --> 00:03:47.826
- Sure, all y'all.

00:03:47.826 --> 00:03:48.659
- You do? Okay.

00:03:48.659 --> 00:03:49.620
- No, I say "all y'all".

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:50.453
- Okay.

00:03:50.453 --> 00:03:52.630
- I mean, I say "y'all"
and I say "all y'all".

00:03:52.630 --> 00:03:56.130
- Yeah, okay, me too. I feel,
okay, I feel cooler now.

00:03:56.130 --> 00:03:57.990
I thought I was going to feel less than

00:03:57.990 --> 00:03:59.340
when you answered that.

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:00.173
(laughs)

00:04:00.173 --> 00:04:02.930
Okay. Saying "Deep in the heart of Texas"

00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:04.343
in front of the Alamo.

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:07.290
- No, I haven't done that. But I will

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:08.970
the next time I go to San Antonio.

00:04:08.970 --> 00:04:11.050
- I know, now it's a challenge,
to both of us I feel,

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:12.320
at this point. I've
never done that either.

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:13.790
I love that that was, I love that people

00:04:13.790 --> 00:04:15.754
in my staff thought that
Texans might do that.

00:04:15.754 --> 00:04:16.920
(laughs)

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:18.253
That's my favorite part.

00:04:19.820 --> 00:04:24.230
Okay. You've gone from
Texas to Texas on vacation.

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:25.690
Have you ever done that?

00:04:25.690 --> 00:04:26.770
- Yes, sure. Of course.

00:04:26.770 --> 00:04:29.410
- Yeah. Just like a road trip?

00:04:29.410 --> 00:04:32.378
- Growing up, we went to El
Paso or we went to San Antonio

00:04:32.378 --> 00:04:36.350
or we went to Houston or, you
know, you just would drive

00:04:36.350 --> 00:04:38.670
from Midland to all
those different places.

00:04:38.670 --> 00:04:40.200
That was your vacation.

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:42.480
- That's so cool. We didn't. Nope.

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:44.390
She was just like, "Have
fun in the backyard.

00:04:44.390 --> 00:04:47.650
In the woods. Create your own adventure."

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:48.700
We didn't do--no, you know,

00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:50.910
we went to Galveston once
when I was a little kid

00:04:50.910 --> 00:04:54.600
and I thought we had hit
the lottery. Yes. I thought,

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:56.860
I thought it was the
most amazing thing ever.

00:04:56.860 --> 00:05:00.400
Okay. Admitted that Fort
Worth is better than Dallas.

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:02.320
You might not, but I will.

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:03.710
- No, I live in Dallas.

00:05:03.710 --> 00:05:04.543
- I know. Well, I'm from

00:05:04.543 --> 00:05:06.540
- I live in Dallas, and Dallas
is better than Fort Worth.

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:07.773
- No, ma'am.

00:05:07.773 --> 00:05:09.454
(laughs)

00:05:09.454 --> 00:05:10.813
No, I'm just kidding. I'm just kidding.

